    "points": [
      {
        "id": "p1",
        "heading": "Week overview",
        "body": "This week we learned to narrate time in Spanish: sometimes the verb travels alone (one word), sometimes with haber (two words: helper + already-done form).\n\nPráctica: Calmly explain to someone at home the difference between canté and he cantado with your own example.\n\nError común: Repeating hard labels without building a real sentence."
      },
      {
        "id": "p2",
        "heading": "Overview: one word",
        "body": "The five one-word times are five stations on the same train: canto, cantaba, canté, cantaré, cantaría.\n\nPráctica: Recite the I-forms and after each one say the «when» in a short phrase.\n\nError común: Using cantaba for a closed yesterday fact."
      },
      {
        "id": "p3",
        "heading": "Overview: two words",
        "body": "The two-word path always has the same shape: helper haber + participle (-ado/-ido). he cantado is the clearest starting example.\n\nPráctica: Write three sentences with he / había / habría + cantado as three moments of one story.\n\nError común: Changing the participle by person."
      },
      {
        "id": "p4",
        "heading": "Overview: choosing",
        "body": "To choose, return to the three questions: closed moment, result until now, or background/habit.\n\nPráctica: Sort five of your sentences into those three groups and explain one aloud.\n\nError común: Choosing at random without looking at time words (ayer, ya, cuando…)."
      },
      {
        "id": "p5",
        "heading": "Synthesis",
        "body": "If you can build an example, you understand the heart of it. Long names can wait; first master the story: one word or two.\n\nPráctica: Write a four-sentence mini-paragraph using at least one one-word time and one two-word time.\n\nError común: Memorizing lists without writing sentences."
      }
    ],
    "summary": ""
  },
